summary:
- topic: FY26 Budget Overview and Challenges
  text: Superintendent Atkinson Duina presented the FY26 budget, highlighting a 5.98%
    increase and a $3.18 million tax increase request. She also updated the board
    on health insurance projections (3-14% increase) and positions previously restored
    to the budget, such as a half-time STEM coordinator, one ESOL teacher, and a percussion
    ed tech.
- topic: Mental Health and DEI Priorities
  text: Superintendent Atkinson Duina emphasized the district's unfinished work in
    mental/behavioral health and Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion (DEI), citing student
    suicides and data showing disparities in student experiences and learning outcomes.
    She advocated for the continuation of the Director of Mental and Behavioral Health
    and Director of DEI positions.
- topic: Board Deliberation on Budget Cuts and Restorations
  text: Board members debated various budget amendments, with several advocating for
    restoring Social Emotional Learning (SEL) positions and the curriculum director.
    Discussions included the financial implications of eliminating the Behavioral
    Health Director and implementing a salary freeze for top administrative staff
    to fund these restorations.
- topic: Public Comment on Staffing and Priorities
  text: Teachers, students, and residents voiced concerns about cuts to student-facing
    positions (SEL, ESOL, Special Education) and the impact on student well-being
    and achievement. Many emphasized the importance of DEI work and questioned the
    rationale behind certain administrative positions versus direct student support
    roles.
- topic: Administrative Salary Freeze Proposal
  text: A key point of contention was a proposal to freeze the salaries of the 12
    highest-paid administrative staff. While some board members and public commenters
    supported this as a measure of shared sacrifice, Superintendent Atkinson Duina
    and other board members strongly opposed it, arguing it would disrespect leaders
    and negatively impact morale.
- topic: Operational Budget Offer from Principals
  text: Rebecca Stern, a middle school principal, offered that building administrators
    could absorb $67,000 from their operational budgets to avoid freezing central
    office salaries. This offer sparked debate among board members about its appropriateness
    and whether it indicated hidden flexibility within the budget.
- topic: Decision to Postpone Budget Vote
  text: The board failed to secure a second for a motion to approve the superintendent's
    budget. Citing ongoing questions, lack of consensus, and new information, the
    board decided to postpone further budget deliberations to a future meeting, acknowledging
    the need for more time to reach a responsible decision.
